Z||000004||bHooper||04-17-2000||12:00 PM||||i have seen photos of the MR2, but havent gotten close enough to one to tell for sure.  it is appealing, i like the fact it looks similar to the boxter.<P>when my GT was running (and my primary car), i looked at traing it 3 times... once for 3rd generation RX7, once for a 2nd generation MR2 and once for a Corrado... and could never pull the strings!  it goes without saying that i am hooked!||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||04-17-2000||02:45 PM||||the MR Spyder may look nice but it's a gutless wonder.. has the LEV motor in it.. not the sweeter GT-S motor that will bolt right in.. why drop 20K on a car that only has 2 things up on most fieros..  (Warranty, and convertable) I've seen a couple up close just recently on my trip to Disney World... those along with the PT Crusier... all show VERY little go.   NAPA gave a PT away.. at least Mopar has a clue and offers a 6psi turbo kit for it... (in stock at most Mopar dealers, at least thats what the sign says) from 150hp(flywheel) to 160hp at the wheels... not too shabby.  but we'll see...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fformula88||04-17-2000||08:26 PM||||The MR2-Spyder isn't going to be quite the as slow as you make it sound.  If it had the Celica GT-S engine, it would be awsome, but Iit should be a capable performer as it is.  It has 138 HP and 125 lb-ft of torque, which isn't earth shattering, but its curb weight is only 2195 LBS.  Of course, the GT-S motor would really sweaten the car.<P>I agre that the PT Cruiser needs more HP.  But give them time.  Remember, the Fiero was only offered with a 4 cyl engine its first year, and it offered a lot less hp than either of these two.  (No offense 4-cyl guys)||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||04-19-2000||03:19 AM||||Break out the Soapbox cuz I got a lota soap to dispense!<P>The MR-2 Spyder doesn't have the Fiero to copy so it copies another good idea, the Boxster. Thanks but no thanks I like being unique and not having my car trying to be something else. Check the numbers on the MR-2. The stock 2.8 still has more torque than it does at a lower rpm and the 3.4s even more. Will people be putting in bigger better engines in their Spyders 15 years later? Hmmm...how many 88 MR-2s do we see with V8s? The Fiero has done what few cars ever do when they are discontinued. Fieros have evolved without help from GM. There are Fieros on the streets that have fulfilled what the factory engineers wanted to do but were told they couldn't do. They built a better Corvette. One with the engine in the middle. Wheather its a 3800 or small block or even a Northstar, Fieros have continued to keep up with all but the newest cars from the imports. Funny, only now the Vette is getting faster than the fastest Fiero? Take the Spyder up against a typical 88 Fiero GT with a 3.4 V6. You mean a 10 year old GM can still take on a brand new Spyder? Hmmm....and what does the Fiero look like today? It didn't have to copy anything already on the road to look beautiful. Fieros don't have to pretend to be something its not. GM was afraid of the potential of the Fiero. The Vette wouldn't have survived a factory V8 Fiero no matter how much it was detuned. <P>The Spyder will most likely be getting the more powerful engine from the Celica in the coming years. Not to worry. There are a lot of Fieros out there that suck the doors off of Mustangs. Our old adversary may have new packaging but still the same copycat content. <P>How "Proud" can they be?<BR>Okay, Ive blown enough hot air now....||||reg||8
Z||000008||Standard||04-19-2000||03:31 AM||||My thoughts exactly!||||reg||14
Z||000009||Bald Dude||04-19-2000||01:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WKDFIRO:<BR><B>GM was afraid of the potential of the Fiero. The Vette wouldn't have survived a factory V8 Fiero no matter how much it was detuned.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This hit the nail right on it's flat head.  As in anything, GM was (and continues to be) plagued by corporate politics and the "good old boy network".  The Fiero was a threat to the Corvette dynasty and they killed it as quickly as possible.  They are _still_ running scared.  Look at the Opel Speedster.  The auto media people have stated that this car would be a great boost to the performance image that Pontiac has tried over the last several years to cultivate.  The engineering has already been completed, the factory could easily build the number of units that GM would need to import.  Hell thay could even build the thing stateside in the abandoned Fiero factory!  An affordable targa top, mid engine sports car that could make GM a ton of money, ready to go and what do they say?  GM says they have no plans to bring this car to the states.  Typical. . .<P>IMHO competition is a good thing.  It makes products better and cheaper.  If Chevrolet  can't (or won't) compete and has to resort to corporate politics to stay alive in the sports car market then we are all diminished.  <P>I bought a new SE in 87.  Sold it in 95 after my kids trashed it and would love to have another one.  If GM ever decides to do the right thing and bring back the Fiero or a suitable replacement the line will form _behind_ me.  Until then I'll keep looking for a good condition 87 SE V6 and I'll keep driving my Miata.<P>I feel much better now.||||reg||1
Z||000010||87PegasusGT||04-19-2000||03:29 PM||||YEAH!!!! What you guys said...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fformula88||04-19-2000||07:55 PM||||Sorry to make everyone so upset.  I like the Fiero better too.  I cannot stand those no-torque little four bangers that you have to rev like crazy to get the power out of.  I just kinda like the design, even if its Boxster inspired.  I also think its nice that Toyota has the guts to build a low cost niche vehicle that so many other companies refuse to do.  In the end, I wouldn't surrender my Fiero to one, but I wouldn't mind owning one either.<p>[This message has been edited by Fformula88 (edited 04-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||RFMike||04-19-2000||08:50 PM||||What really blows me away is that GM built the Fiero of today way back in 1984.  That's right, this was a car that would easily do 140 mph because of its 232 hp four cylinder engine, was extremely stable at that speed because of its aerodynamic design, had great cornering and handling capabilities because of polyurethane bushings and solid aluminum engine mounts.  <BR>In short, it was a hell of a car, one that we dream about.  It was the 1984 Indy Pace Car (the Real one).  This is the straight stuff, right out of the Hot Rod Magazine Indianoplis test drive article. This could have been produced in 1984, and evolved all the way to today.  Man, oh man, oh man.  What might have been.||||reg||1
